WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:10.850
My name is Joe DIY.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.113
I restore old toy trucks,

00:00:12.113 --> 00:00:13.500
and today I'm going to
be walking you through

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.803
the restoration of a 1960s
Structo airport lift truck.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.880
These toys are considered
pressed-steel toys.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.940
That means they take
a sheet metal aluminum

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.500
and they use a hydraulic press

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.420
to kind of form it into shape.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.740
The truck's pretty rusty.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.040
It's mostly complete, which is good,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.810
'cause they're hard to find parts for.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.952
The only thing that's really missing

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:33.860
was a lot of the lights and mirrors

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.443
that you'll see on the roof.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.540
Whenever I take things apart,

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.550
I always start at the wheels,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:41.718
and then I'll move on

00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:43.840
and start taking the body off the chassis

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.470
and kind of work my way
down to the chassis.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.313
Usually that's the last
part that will be together.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:51.383
The reason I do this is

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.111
it's just easier to work
from the outside in.

00:00:54.111 --> 00:00:55.870
Everything is attached to that chassis,

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.140
so you need to get off, you know,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.540
piece by piece and just
work your way down there.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.660
Here I'm using a rotary tool

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.770
to grind off the end of the rivet.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:05.686
When rivets are put in,

00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:07.352
they're kind of mushroomed at the end

00:01:07.352 --> 00:01:08.500
so that they won't fall out.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.680
So you need to take off that ridge

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.020
so that you can pull the rivet out

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.860
and allow the pieces to come off.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.700
When I take them apart,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.330
I use a lot of different tools.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.760
A screwdriver and pliers
are the main tools I use.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.493
Usually you can use the screwdriver

00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:22.526
to bend out the tab a little bit,

00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:24.140
and then once you get
it away from the toy,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.313
then you can use your
pliers to fully bend it out.

00:01:26.313 --> 00:01:28.310
And those tabs are what's going to hold

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.500
a lot of the parts together,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.780
so it allows you to get those tabs out

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.233
and slide the truck apart.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.040
Once the truck's completely disassembled,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.320
I take it over to my sandblasting booth

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.470
and I try to remove
all the paint and rust.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.160
Likely, the rust came
from, just over time,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.685
this toy being played with,

00:01:49.685 --> 00:01:51.085
probably left outside,

00:01:51.085 --> 00:01:52.930
it would get beat up, it
would get paint chips.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.940
So anytime you have exposed steel to air

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.560
and to moisture in the air and oxygen,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.273
that's when you get rust forming.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.090
I'm removing the rust with a sandblaster.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.230
It's a sandblast cabinet,

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:06.380
and the media inside of it

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.010
is spraying out aluminum oxide.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.940
So you can think of it like
a really abrasive powder

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.713
that gets shot through the gun.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:15.750
So you get a lot of air behind it,

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.180
and it's just blowing it at
that toy as hard as it can,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.407
and that abrasive material
will take off any rust,

00:02:20.407 --> 00:02:22.540
paint, or anything
that's left on the metal.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.550
You have to remove all the rust before

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.350
you repaint it or powder-coat it.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.510
Any rust or oxide that's
left underneath that layer

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.313
will eventually continue to rust

00:02:32.313 --> 00:02:33.640
and come through your paint

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.170
or come through your powder coat,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.820
or it may not even stick
properly in the first place.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.150
Well, we're down to bare metal.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.520
Now we can go ahead and
powder-coat our parts.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.010
For powder coating, I use
an Eastwood powder coater.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.520
Pretty much what this machine does

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.481
is it takes powdered plastic

00:02:52.481 --> 00:02:53.456
and it mixes with air

00:02:53.456 --> 00:02:55.260
and sprays the powder at the parts.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.200
And then there's an electrical charge

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.100
inside the powder-coating gun,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.300
which is going to discharge

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.090
with the powder in the air

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.973
and pretty much electrically charge

00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:05.381
that cloud of powder.

00:03:05.381 --> 00:03:08.310
That electrified air wants
to find the closest ground,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.370
and that's what causes
it to stick to the part.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.620
So, as you watch it get powder-coated,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.200
you'll see that the cloud of dust

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.723
just clings to the metal.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.198
After it's coated,

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.056
I put it in my little electric oven.

00:03:20.056 --> 00:03:22.570
It gets up to about
400 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.330
And what that's going to do
is melt down that powder.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.980
And that's where you see it change color.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.460
It'll go from a dull
color to a glossy color.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.920
That's called the flow.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.700
Once it flows out, then
you allow it to cure,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.200
and that's what's going to
harden the color on there.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.430
All said and done, powder
coating's very quick.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.350
From putting it in the oven

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:41.900
till the time you can
take it out and handle it,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.741
it's about 15, 20 minutes.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:49.930
So now I'm going to go ahead

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.330
and just clean the tires.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.110
I'm only washing the black
rubber part of the tire,

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.576
because I'm just going to clean these up

00:03:55.576 --> 00:03:57.620
and use them as is.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.830
I just put this in a
solution of Simple Green

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.340
inside my ultrasonic cleaner,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.150
and I'm running it at
about 40 degrees Celsius

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.500
for about 20 minutes,
just vibrating the parts.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.226
And all that's going to do

00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:09.526
is just remove any of the dirt

00:04:09.526 --> 00:04:11.550
and crud that's caught in the crevices

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.513
and the tread on the tires.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.210
So now the white-walled side
of the tire is all stained,

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:19.510
so I need to get this
back to the white color.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.260
I put this in a mix of
hydrogen peroxide and bleach,

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.710
and then you want to
expose it to UV light.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.068
So I usually just put it outside

00:04:27.068 --> 00:04:28.243
in the sun for a few days,

00:04:28.243 --> 00:04:30.893
and that bleaches it back
to the original white color.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:36.580
This truck is a little rare

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.810
because of the moving body on the back.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.680
That makes it a little uncommon.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.930
I know a lot of people out there

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.030
seek these kind of operational vehicles,

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.520
the ones that have a lot
of moving parts on them.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.280
So the most difficult
part of the restoration

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.113
is definitely going to be

00:04:48.113 --> 00:04:49.362
putting this thing back together.

00:04:49.362 --> 00:04:52.050
There are a lot of rivets
located underneath the body

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.446
that need to all be in place

00:04:53.446 --> 00:04:56.440
so that the body will
move up and down properly

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.900
and everything will function as it should.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.700
Once I have everything
cleaned up and painted,

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.940
I'll usually start by assembling the cab.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.440
I usually do this because
it's got the windshield,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.115
and it's just a nice, easy part

00:05:08.115 --> 00:05:10.165
that I can just start to assemble

00:05:10.165 --> 00:05:12.107
before I start moving on to the chassis.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.770
So, when I'm putting it back together,

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.770
I try to use the original style rivets.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:17.970
And then I use a special tool

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.730
that'll crimp them in the same style

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.630
that this would've had from the factory.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.160
The two main tools I use for reassembling

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.600
is a clamp tool called the Pintonka tool,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.870
and that just crushes the
original style rivets.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.170
It's specially designed just for Tonkas

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.210
and pinball machines.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.580
And the other tool is
a small brass hammer.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.930
One side of the head's brass,
the other side's nylon,

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.820
and that allows me to bend the tabs

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.495
without marring any of the paintwork.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.690
The logos are reproduction
stickers I find online.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:06.440
There's a bunch of reproduction companies

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:07.910
out there that make them,

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.036
and I just get them from
there and reapply them.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.760
And after all that, it's fully assembled

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.460
and ready to be played with again.

